%[text] # Stability Assessment of Data Center Power Delivery
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] This study assesses stability of a data center power delivery network under firmware faults, weak grid conditions, and Uninterruptible Power Supply (UPS) controller degradation. Four simulation cases demonstrate independent failure modes:
%[text] 1. **Firmware Bug** — degraded Power Factor Correction (PFC) gains ($K\_p \< K\_{p,crit}$) amplify normal Graphics Processing Unit (GPU) workload fluctuations into forced resonance.
%[text] 2. **Bypass + Weak Grid** — UPS bypassed with Short Circuit Ratio (SCR) = 3 exposes PFCs to unfiltered grid disturbances.
%[text] 3. **SCR Sweep** — Point of Common Coupling (PCC) voltage flicker under weak grid (SCR = 10, 5, 3) with UPS connected.
%[text] 4. **Degraded AFE** — weakened UPS Active Front-End (AFE) rectifier controller lets PFC oscillation leak onto the 800V DC bus. \
%[text] Analytical results (natural frequencies, stability map, impedance-based penetration limit) frame the simulation findings. 
%%
%[text] ## System Description
%[text] The Simulink model [`DataCenterStability`](file:.\StabilityAssessment\DataCenterStability.slx) represents an 8 MW data center fed from a utility grid through a double-conversion UPS. The code below loads component parameters from `DataCenterParamScan`, selects the GPU training workload profile (G1=1), distributes 400 PFC units across three parallel blocks, and constructs a `StabilityStudy` object that maps every Simulink block path, Simscape log signal, and mask parameter to its role in the analysis.
%[text] - **400 PFC units** (20 kW each) in 3 blocks, fed by double-conversion UPS (800V DC, 480V AC).
%[text] - **5184 GPUs** (3 racks, 216 trays/rack, 8 GPU/tray) running Large Language Model (LLM) training workload (G1=1).
%[text] - Grid impedance  (runtime-tunable).
%[text] - UPS bypass controlled by circuit breaker blocks (main switch, by-pass switch). \
%%
  %[control:button:50e2]{"position":[1,2]}
open_system("DataCenterStability");
%%
%[text] 
%[text] ![](text:image:0021)
% Load all component parameters (PFC, UPS, generator, IT tray, grid, LVRT)
DataCenterParamScan;

% G1=1 activates GPU compute profile; G2=0 disables constant-power path
G1 = 1; G2 = 0;

% 400 PFC units split equally across 3 parallel blocks (A, B, C)
N_total = 400;
N_pfc = round(N_total * [1/3, 1/3, 1/3]);
N_pfc(3) = N_total - N_pfc(1) - N_pfc(2);  % absorb rounding remainder

% Create the StabilityStudy object with full system configuration
s = StabilityStudy(...
    ModelName="DataCenterStability", ...                                     % Simulink model name
    PFCBlocks=["DataCenterStability/PFC A", "DataCenterStability/PFC B", "DataCenterStability/PFC C"], ... % 3 PFC subsystems
    UPSBlock="DataCenterStability/UPS", ...                                  % UPS subsystem path
    CableBlock="DataCenterStability/Cable A", ...                            % AC cable block for impedance
    RLCBlock="DataCenterStability/Measurement A/Measurements/RLC (Three-Phase)", ... % Grid impedance (tunable R,L)
    ITTrayBlocks=["DataCenterStability/IT  Load A", "DataCenterStability/IT Load B", "DataCenterStability/IT Load C"], ... % IT load blocks
    SimlogPaths=struct( ...                                                % Simscape logging paths
        'pfc_vdc', {{"PFC_A.v_dc", "PFC_B.v_dc", "PFC_C.v_dc"}}, ...       %   PFC DC bus voltages
        'pfc_iac', {{"PFC_A.i_ac", "PFC_B.i_ac", "PFC_C.i_ac"}}, ...       %   PFC AC currents
        'ups_vdc', "UPS.DC_Link.C1.vc"), ...                               %   UPS 800V DC link voltage
    BlockParams=struct( ...                                                % Mask parameter names for runtime tuning
        'Kp', "Kp_pfc", 'Ki', "Ki_pfc", ...                                %   PFC voltage loop gains
        'C_dc', "C_dc", 'G_damp', "G_damp", ...                            %   DC bus capacitance & damping
        'capacitance', "capacitance", ...                                  %   UPS DC bus capacitance
        'kiDC', "kiDC", ...                                                %   UPS DC bus integral gain
        'R', "R", 'L', "L"), ...                                           %   RLC grid impedance R and L
    OperatingPoint=struct('V_dc', 400, 'V_nom', 277, 'V_ups', 800, 'eta', 0.97, 'f_line', 60, 'XR', 10), ... % Nominal voltages, efficiency, X/R ratio
    Load=struct('P_total', N_total * pfc.P_max * 1e6, 'P_facility', 8e6, 'N_pfc_facility', 400), ...         % 8 MW total facility load
    Controller=struct('Kp', pfc.Kp, 'Ki', pfc.Ki, 'C', pfc.C_dc, 'G_damp', pfc.G_damp), ...                  % Nominal PFC controller gains
    Topology=struct('N_total', N_total, 'N_pfc', N_pfc), ...                                                 % PFC block distribution
    ITTray=struct('numRack', itRack.numRack, 'nGPU', itTray.nGPU, 'nCPU', itTray.nCPU, 'nTray', nTray, 'dcdc_P_rated', itTray.dcdc.P_rated, 'dcdc_eff', itTray.dcdc.eff, 'Ngpu', Ngpu), ... % IT rack/tray/GPU topology
    UPSController=struct('Kp', ups.AFE.kpVoltage, 'Ki', ups.AFE.kiVoltage, 'C', ups.DCBus.capacitance, 'V_dc', ups.Vdc), ... % UPS AFE controller params
    AFEController=struct('Kp', ups.AFE.kpVoltage, 'Ki', ups.AFE.kiVoltage), ...  % AFE gains (for degradation case)
    SetupScript="DataCenterParamScan", ...                                       % Re-run this script before each sim
    AddPath="..");                                                               % Add parent folder to path

%%
%[text] ## 1. Analytical Predictions
%[text] Before running any simulation, you derive the stability boundary of the PFC voltage control loop from first principles. This analytical step establishes what to expect from the simulations and provides a simple pass/fail criterion for firmware validation.
%[text] ### 1.1 PFC Voltage Loop as a Second-Order System
%[text] Each PFC unit regulates its DC bus voltage $V\_{dc}$ to a 400 V setpoint using a Proportional-Integral (PI) controller that commands real power drawn from the AC grid. From the DC bus perspective, the GPU load behind the LLC (resonant DC-DC converter) stage behaves as a Constant-Power Load (CPL): it draws fixed power $P$ regardless of bus voltage. This CPL presents a negative incremental impedance ($Z = -V^2/P$) that opposes the controller's damping effort.
%[text] Linearizing around the operating point ($V\_0 = 400$ V, $P = 20$ kW per PFC), the closed-loop DC bus voltage dynamics reduce to a standard second-order transfer function with:
%[text] - **Natural frequency** — set by the integral gain and bus capacitance: \
%[text]  $f\_n = \\sqrt{K\_i/(C \\cdot V\_0)}/(2\\pi)$$$
%[text] - **Damping ratio** — the competition between controller gain and CPL negative resistance: \
%[text]  $\\zeta = \\frac{K\_p/V\_0 - P/V\_0^2}{2\\sqrt{C \\cdot K\_i / V\_0}}$$$
%[text] The numerator of $\\zeta$ reveals the stability boundary: damping is positive only when $K\_p/V\_0 \> P/V\_0^2$, i.e., when the proportional gain exceeds the critical threshold:
%[text] $K\_{p,crit} = P/V\_0 = 20{,}000/400 = 50 \\text{ W/V}$$$. Below this threshold the CPL's negative damping wins and the DC bus voltage oscillates at $f\_n$. Above it, the controller provides positive damping and the system is stable.
%[text] ### 1.2 Nominal vs. Buggy Controller
%[text] The table below compares the nominal PFC gains against a hypothetical firmware bug that ships degraded gains:
T_nominal_vs_buggy = table( ...
    categorical(["Nominal"; "Buggy"]), ...
    [200; 33], [600; 600], [0.88; -0.10], [1.13; 1.13], ...
    categorical(["4x above"; "34% below"]), ...
    VariableNames=["Controller", "Kp (W/V)", "Ki (W/(V*s))", ...
    "Damping Ratio", "Natural Freq (Hz)", "Margin to Kp crit"]);
disp(T_nominal_vs_buggy) %[output:9f4dd024]
%[text] Key observations:
%[text] - The nominal controller is nearly critically damped ($\\zeta = 0.88$) with a 4× safety margin over $K\_{p,crit}$. Normal GPU workload fluctuations are well attenuated.
%[text] - The buggy controller ($K\_p = 33 \< 50$) has *negative* damping. It does not merely reduce stability margin — it makes the system actively unstable. Any load perturbation (even normal GPU utilization changes) will be amplified into sustained oscillation at ~1.1 Hz.
%[text] - A firmware error that reduces $K\_p$ by 75% (e.g., wrong scaling factor, misplaced decimal) would cross this boundary. This is a plausible failure mode. \
%[text] ### 1.3 Closed-Loop Output Impedance
%[text] The closed-loop output impedance:
%[text] $Z\_{cl}(s) = s,/,(C s^2 + (K\_p/V\_0 - P/V\_0^2),s + K\_i/V\_0)$
%[text] quantifies how much DC bus voltage ripple results from a unit load-power perturbation at each frequency. The Bode plot overlays nominal ($K\_p = 200$, blue) and buggy ($K\_p = 33$, orange) responses. 
%[text] The shaded 0.5–3 Hz region marks the CPL critical band where the resonant peak sits.
%[text] - **Nominal (blue):** Broad, low peak (~6 dB) at $f\_{res} \\approx 1.13$ Hz — well-damped ($\\zeta = 0.88$). GPU workload fluctuations are attenuated. Phase transitions smoothly through 0°.
%[text] - **Buggy (orange):** Sharp, tall peak (~30+ dB) at the same frequency — negative damping ($\\zeta = -0.10$) amplifies disturbances instead of rejecting them. Phase shows a steep 180° flip, the signature of oscillatory instability.
%[text] - **Above ~10 Hz:** Both curves roll off — the SISO model is blind to higher-frequency modes (PLL at 7–8 Hz, AC filter at 19–20 Hz), which require the MIMO PRBS scan. \
% Compute analytical predictions: natural frequencies, damping, Z_cl Bode plot
s.runSetup(); %[output:0c13fb11]
%%
%[text] **Table 1: Nominal Controller Frequency Response** — natural frequency, damping ratio, resonant frequency, and critical proportional gain for the healthy PFC voltage loop.
disp(s.Setup.T_freq) %[output:797c6042]
%%
%[text] **Table 2: Buggy Controller Parameters** — with $K\_p = 33 \< K\_{p,crit} = 50$, the damping ratio is negative ($\\zeta = -0.10$), confirming analytical instability.
disp(s.Setup.T_buggy) %[output:4c1308c6]
%%
%[text] ### 1.4 Limitations of the Linear Model
%[text] The predictions above are derived from small-signal linearization at a fixed operating point ($V\_0 = 400$ V).
%[text] When the system is actually unstable, the oscillation grows to large amplitude (~54 Vpp, ±6.7% of $V\_0$), where two nonlinearities become significant:
%[text] - The CPL's negative impedance ($Z = -V^2/P$) is amplitude-dependent at large voltage swings
%[text] - The PFC boost converter transitions between Continuous and Discontinuous Conduction Modes (CCM/DCM), changing the effective loop gain.  \
%[text] These effects may shift the observed resonant frequency away from the predicted $f\_n$ — typically by 10–20%.
%[text] The analytical model is therefore best used for predicting the **stability boundary** ($K\_{p,crit}$) and the **approximate frequency band**, not the exact oscillation frequency. Sections 3–5 validate these predictions against time-domain simulation.
%%
%[text] ## 2. Baseline (Healthy Operation)
%[text] A single time-domain simulation with all nominal parameters (healthy PFC gains, UPS online, strong grid) establishes the reference waveform. The model runs the GPU training workload and records PFC DC bus voltage, UPS DC link voltage, and AC currents. Metrics extracted from the steady-state window (last 2 s) include DC bus voltage range, peak-to-peak ripple, and standard deviation. These serve as the "healthy" benchmark against which all fault cases are compared.
% Simulate healthy system (nominal gains, UPS online, strong grid) as reference
s.runBaseline(); %[output:759d21fc]
%%
%[text] **Baseline steady-state metrics** — PFC DC bus and UPS DC link voltage range under nominal operation. The PFC ripple (peak-to-peak) and standard deviation establish the healthy reference; any fault case producing significantly larger values indicates degraded stability. The UPS DC link (800 V nominal) should show minimal ripple when the AFE controller is healthy.
disp(s.Baseline.T_metrics) %[output:043094de]
%%
%[text] ## 3. Four-Case Stability Demonstration
%[text] The analytical predictions in Section 1 established that $K\_p \< K\_{p,crit} = 50$ W/V produces negative damping, and that the resulting oscillation should appear near $f\_n \\approx 1.13$ Hz. This section validates those predictions with time-domain simulations and extends the analysis to three additional failure modes that the linear model does not cover (grid-coupled disturbances, UPS controller degradation).
%[text] Four independent failure modes are simulated, each isolating one variable while keeping the rest at nominal. After simulation, `StabilityStudy` extracts Simscape logs, computes per-case metrics (DC bus ripple, Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) peak frequency, PCC flicker), and generates comparison plots.
%[text] **Case 1 — Firmware Bug**: Degraded PFC A ($K\_p = 33$, $K\_i = 600$, $\\zeta \< 0$) with UPS online. Section 1 predicts this controller is unstable with oscillation near 1.13 Hz. The simulation confirms sustained forced resonance driven by normal GPU workload fluctuations. The UPS cannot compensate for a local controller instability.
%[text] **Case 2 — Bypass + Weak Grid**: UPS bypassed at SCR = 3 with healthy PFCs. Grid disturbances (harmonics, voltage fluctuation) propagate directly to PFC DC buses without UPS filtering.
%[text] **Case 3 — SCR Sweep**: UPS connected, healthy PFCs, SCR = \[10, 5, 3\]. Quantifies PCC voltage flicker — UPS shields PFCs from grid-side disturbances.
%[text] **Case 4 — Degraded AFE**: Healthy PFCs with weakened UPS rectifier controller ($K\_p = 8$, $K\_i = 120$, 10% of nominal). Normal GPU workload fluctuations produce larger UPS DC bus ripple — the degraded AFE cannot reject load-induced disturbances.
% Run all four failure-mode cases (firmware bug, bypass, SCR sweep, degraded AFE)
s.runUPSBypassDemo(SCR_sweep=[10, 5, 3]); %[output:220350b1] %[output:7e91039f] %[output:3ca83d76] %[output:3bee48f2] %[output:2e310f7c]
%%
%[text] ### Case Comparison
%[text] **Table 5: Case Comparison** — DC bus ripple and dominant oscillation frequency across all failure cases. The `Dominant Oscillation (Hz)` column reports the FFT peak frequency from each case's PFC DC bus voltage waveform.
disp(s.UPSBypassDemo.T_comparison) %[output:96c8a38f]
%%
%[text] **Validation against analytical prediction:**
fprintf('Observed oscillation: %.2f Hz | Predicted: %.2f Hz\n', s.UPSBypassDemo.firmware.f_osc, s.Setup.f_n_buggy) %[output:07e359c8]
%%
%[text] The firmware-bug case (Case 1) oscillates at the frequency predicted by the linearized model, confirming the $K\_{p,crit}$ stability boundary. The ~20% discrepancy between predicted (1.13 Hz) and observed (~1.35 Hz) frequency is consistent with the linearization limitations discussed in Section 1.4 — large-signal nonlinearities shift the resonance upward from the small-signal prediction.
%[text] Key observations from the table:
%[text] - **Case 1 (Firmware Bug)** shows 2$\\times$ the DC bus ripple of the healthy cases (53.8 V vs ~25 V peak-to-peak), directly caused by the negative damping predicted in Section 1.2. The oscillation is self-sustaining — it does not decay.
%[text] - **Case 2 (Bypass + Weak Grid)** and **Case 4 (Degraded AFE)** show low-frequency oscillation content (0.30 Hz and 0.55 Hz respectively) but with ripple comparable to the healthy baseline (~25 V). These are disturbance-driven, not instability-driven — the PFC controllers remain stable ($\\zeta \> 0$) and the oscillation does not grow.
%[text] - The contrast confirms the analytical insight: *controller instability* (negative $\\zeta$) is qualitatively different from *disturbance amplification* (positive $\\zeta$ with external forcing). The former is self-sustaining regardless of input; the latter scales with the disturbance amplitude. \
%%
%[text] ### PCC Voltage Flicker (SCR Sweep)
%[text] For SCR = 10, 5, and 3, the PCC Root Mean Square (RMS) voltage is logged and flicker is computed as the peak-to-peak variation in the last 2 s expressed as a percentage of nominal. Lower SCR means higher grid impedance, so voltage swings at the PCC increase — but the UPS shields the downstream PFC DC buses from these fluctuations.
%[text] **Table 6: PCC Voltage Flicker vs Grid SCR** — voltage swing, flicker percentage, PFC ripple, and UPS ripple at each SCR level. Status thresholds: OK (\< 3%), IEC LIMIT (3–8%), SEVERE (\> 8%).
disp(s.UPSBypassDemo.T_scr) %[output:05f2efef]
%%
%[text] ### AFE Degradation — UPS DC Bus Impact
%[text] The UPS active front-end (AFE) rectifier regulates the 800 V DC link. When its controller gains are reduced to 10% of nominal ($K\_p = 8$, $K\_i = 120$), the AFE can no longer reject load-induced power fluctuations from the PFC side. The table compares the 800 V DC bus ripple under nominal vs degraded AFE gains — a direct measure of how controller degradation turns the UPS into an amplifier rather than a buffer.
%[text] **Table 7: AFE Controller Degradation** — UPS 800 V DC bus ripple and controller bandwidth under nominal vs degraded AFE gains. Status thresholds: OK (\< 10 Vpp), WARNING (10–40 Vpp), SEVERE (\> 40 Vpp).
disp(s.UPSBypassDemo.T_afe) %[output:1676f49e]
%%
%[text] ## 4. Stability Map
%[text] The stability map sweeps the proportional gain $K\_p$ from 0 to 350 W/V and computes the damping ratio $\\zeta(K\_p)$ analytically using $K\_i = 600$ W/(V·s). Since the firmware bug only changes $K\_p$ (not $K\_i$), both nominal and buggy controllers lie on the same $\\zeta(K\_p)$ curve. The plot marks two operating points: the nominal controller ($K\_p = 200$, $\\zeta = 0.88$) well inside the stable region, and the buggy controller ($K\_p = 33$, $\\zeta = -0.10$) inside the shaded unstable region below $K\_{p,crit} = 50$ W/V. This provides a single-glance visual for firmware validation — any deployed PFC gain must lie to the right of the boundary.
% Sweep Kp from 0 to 350 — plot damping ratio zeta vs Kp, mark Kp_crit boundary
s.runStabilityMap(); %[output:9ee89780]
%%
%[text] ## 5. Firmware Penetration Limit
%[text] In a fleet of 400 PFC units, what happens if a firmware update with degraded gains ($K\_p = 33$) is rolled out to a fraction of the fleet?
%[text]  The impedance-based Nyquist scan answers this by constructing a composite load admittance that blends healthy and buggy PFC impedances at varying ratios (0–100%) and forming the loop gain $L(s) = Z\_{grid,dc}(s) \\cdot Y\_{total}(s)$, where $Z\_{grid,dc}$ is the grid impedance reflected to the DC side at SCR = 10. 
%[text] For each ratio two metrics are computed: (1) the **winding number** around $-1$ (zero = stable, nonzero = unstable), and (2) the **minimum distance** to $-1$ (a robustness measure — smaller distance means poorer damping and larger oscillations). 
%[text] A margin threshold (default 0.9) flags the **marginal** penetration fraction where damping degrades enough to cause poorly damped oscillations, well before formal instability. The critical fraction is where the Nyquist contour actually encircles $-1$.
%[text] ### MIMO Admittance Scan
%[text] While the Single-Input Single-Output (SISO) impedance analysis above uses an analytical PFC model, the Multiple-Input Multiple-Output (MIMO) admittance scan measures the actual 2$\\times$2 DQ-frame admittance matrix $Y(s)$ directly from the Simulink model via Pseudo-Random Binary Sequence (PRBS) injection.
%[text]  Small-signal perturbations are injected on the D and Q voltage axes at three load levels (50%, 70%, 90%) and the resulting current responses are processed via spectral analysis to extract $Y\_{dd}$, $Y\_{dq}$, $Y\_{qd}$, $Y\_{qq}$. This captures cross-coupling and nonlinear effects that the analytical model cannot. 
%[text] The scan runs 6 parallel simulations (~15 min total). By default, pre-computed results are loaded from `gnc_results.mat`. Tick the checkbox to run a fresh scan. \\
% Extract configuration and create GNC analyzer for MIMO stability
cfg = s.Cfg;
gnc = GNCAnalyzer(cfg);
gncResultsPath = fullfile(fileparts(which('StabilityStudy')), 'AdmittanceScan', 'gnc_results.mat');
%%
if false % Tick to run fresh PRBS admittance scan %[control:checkbox:25b0]{"position":[4,9]}
    % Inject PRBS on D and Q axes at 50%, 70%, 90% load — 6 parsim runs
    gnc.runAdmittanceScan(U_levels=[0.5, 0.7, 0.9], Axes=["D-axis", "Q-axis"]); %#ok<UNRCH>
    Y_all = gnc.Admittance.Y_all;           % 2x2 DQ admittance per load level
    save(gncResultsPath, 'Y_all');
    fprintf('Admittance scan complete — saved to gnc_results.mat\n');
else
    % Load pre-computed admittance matrices (skip PRBS scan)
    Y_gnc = load(gncResultsPath, 'Y_all'); 
    Y_all = Y_gnc.Y_all;
end
%%
%[text] ### **MIMO GNC Stability Assessment (AC-Side Grid-Load Interaction)**
%[text] Does the grid-load interaction at the 480V PCC cause instability?
%[text] **Why a PRBS scan is needed:** The analytical PFC model (used in SISO) captures only the 1 Hz DC bus mode. The scan reveals dynamics invisible to analytics:
%[text] - Q-axis admittance resonance at **7–8 Hz** (PLL, reactive power loop)
%[text] - D-axis admittance peak at **19–20 Hz** (AC filter, active power)
%[text] - GNC eigenvalue interaction peak at $\\sim$**9.6 Hz** \
%[text] **Method:** GNC forms $L(j\\omega) = Z\_{grid} \\cdot Y\_{load}$ and tracks two eigenvalue loci ($\\lambda\_1$, $\\lambda\_2$). 
%[text] Zero encirclements of $-1$ $\\Rightarrow$ stable; minimum distance to $-1$ $\\Rightarrow$ robustness.
%[text] **Caveat:** PRBS was measured with nominal firmware ($K\_p = 200$). The buggy PFC ($K\_p = 33$) diverges at full load and cannot be scanned — combined buggy-firmware + weak-grid behavior is uncharacterized.
%[text] **Classification:** Status (phase margin: STABLE \> 45°, MARGINAL 15–45°, CRITICAL \< 15°) and Robustness (distance to $-1$: Robust $\\geq$ 0.9, Marginal 0.5–0.9, Poor $\<$ 0.5).
% Evaluate GNC at SCR = 10, 5, 3 using 90% load admittance (worst case)
SCR_gnc = [10, 5, 3];
gnc_mimo = GNCAnalyzer.computeLoopGainMIMO(Y_all{end}, SCR_gnc, cfg);  % Y_all{end} = 90% load
disp(gnc_mimo.T_stability) %[output:85c50773]
%[text] **Eigenvalue Nyquist Loci** — Both $\\lambda\_1$, $\\lambda\_2$ of $L(j\\omega) = Z\_{grid} \\cdot Y\_{load}$ for each SCR. Red $\\times$ = critical point $-1$.
%[text] - No encirclement of $-1$ $\\Rightarrow$ stable; clockwise encirclements = unstable poles
%[text] - Distance $d$ to $-1$ = damping margin — smaller $d$ means poorer damping even if stable \
GNCAnalyzer.plotEigNyquist(gnc_mimo, SCR_gnc); %[output:8f21b6ff]
%[text] **Eigenvalue Bode** — Solid = $\\lambda\_1$, dashed = $\\lambda\_2$. Phase margin is read where magnitude crosses 0 dB.
%[text] - **SCR = 5, 3:** Loop gain well above 0 dB in 3–20 Hz with phase near $-180\\degree $ $\\Rightarrow$ unstable
%[text] - **SCR = 10:** Below 0 dB with adequate phase margin $\\Rightarrow$ stable \
GNCAnalyzer.plotEigBode(gnc_mimo, SCR_gnc); %[output:8678282f]
%[text] **Utilization Sweep** — Top row: $\\lambda\_1$, bottom row: $\\lambda\_2$, across 50/70/90% load.
%[text] - Higher utilization pushes loci outward, closer to $-1$
%[text] - **SCR = 10:** Stable at all loads, but robustness degrades with utilization
%[text] - **SCR = 5, 3:** Unstable at all loads; loci grow dramatically at 90% \
U_pct = [50, 70, 90];
gnc_sweep = cell(1, numel(Y_all));
for u = 1:numel(Y_all)
    gnc_sweep{u} = GNCAnalyzer.computeLoopGainMIMO(Y_all{u}, SCR_gnc, cfg);
end
GNCAnalyzer.plotUtilSweep(gnc_sweep, SCR_gnc, U_pct); %[output:3e41e88a]
%%
%[text] **Table 8: GNC MIMO Utilization Sweep Summary** — Stability status, phase margin, and minimum distance to $-1$ across load levels and grid SCR. The system is stable only at SCR = 10; weaker grids (SCR $\\leq$ 5) are unstable regardless of load level.
T_sweep = cellfun(@(r) r.T_stability, gnc_sweep, 'UniformOutput', false);
T_sweep = vertcat(T_sweep{:});
T_sweep = addvars(T_sweep, repelem(U_pct(:), numel(SCR_gnc)), 'Before', 1, 'NewVariableNames', 'Load (%)');
disp(T_sweep) %[output:677dedbc]
%[text] **Key findings:**
%[text] 1. SCR $\\geq$ 10 required for MIMO stability at all load levels
%[text] 2. At SCR = 10: robustness degrades from Marginal (50% load) to Poor (90%) — stable but limited damping
%[text] 3. SCR = 5 and 3: unstable at every load level (1 and 2 encirclements respectively)
%[text] 4. SISO and MIMO are complementary: SISO $\\rightarrow$ firmware rollout limit (1–3 Hz, grid-independent); MIMO $\\rightarrow$ grid strength requirement (7–20 Hz, firmware-independent). Both must be satisfied. \
%%
%[text] ### **SISO Impedance Penetration Scan (DC-Side Firmware Fleet Stability)**
%[text] How many buggy firmware units before the 400V DC bus goes unstable?
%[text] - Each degraded PFC ($K\_p = 33$) adds negative incremental resistance to the DC bus
%[text] - When aggregate negative conductance exceeds bus capacitance damping $\\Rightarrow$ CPL oscillation at $\\sim$1 Hz
%[text] - At $\\sim$80% penetration the Nyquist locus approaches $-1$ at 2–3 Hz (shifted up from 1.13 Hz natural frequency) \
%[text] **Method:** Loop gain $L(s) = Z\_{PFC}(s) \\cdot P\_{total}/V^2$ — analytical, models only the voltage controller loop. Grid-independent (AC impedance does not appear). Does not capture the 7–20 Hz modes (PLL, AC filter, DQ coupling) that the PRBS-based MIMO GNC addresses.
s.runImpedanceScan(); %[output:32081a77] %[output:6f15fc37] %[output:5de22821]
%%
% Report the maximum safe percentage of degraded firmware units
fprintf('Marginal (poorly damped): %.0f%% (%d / %d units)\n', s.ImpedanceScan.frac_marginal*100, s.ImpedanceScan.N_marginal, s.Cfg.Topology.N_total) %[output:0b3b1c52]
fprintf('Critical (unstable): %.0f%% (%d / %d units)\n', s.ImpedanceScan.frac_critical*100, s.ImpedanceScan.N_critical, s.Cfg.Topology.N_total) %[output:70d8d7bc]
%%
%[text] ### **SISO vs MIMO — Complementary Stability Tests**
T_comparison = table( ...
    categorical(["Location"; "Frequency band"; "Model"; "Swept variable"; "Assumes nominal"]), ...
    ["400V DC bus"; "1–3 Hz"; "Analytical (PFC voltage loop)"; "Buggy firmware fraction (0–100%)"; "Grid (infinite SCR)"], ...
    ["480V AC PCC"; "7–20 Hz"; "PRBS-measured (2x2 DQ admittance)"; "Grid SCR (3, 5, 10)"; sprintf("Firmware (Kp = %d)", s.Cfg.Controller.Kp)], ...
    VariableNames=["Attribute", "SISO", "MIMO GNC"]);
disp(T_comparison) %[output:7af67643]
%[text] Each is blind to the other's mode: SISO $|L|$ at 9.6 Hz $\\ll 1$; GNC $|\\lambda|$ at 1.13 Hz $\\ll 1$. Non-overlapping bands confirm physically distinct mechanisms.h
%[text] **Limitation:** The combined worst case (buggy firmware + weak grid) is uncharacterized — the buggy PFC diverges at full load and cannot be scanned. Individual thresholds (firmware $\<$ 80%, SCR $\\geq$ 10) are necessary but may not be jointly sufficient.
%%
%[text] ## Conclusion
%[text] Six risk scenarios across four failure categories:
T_conclusion = table( ...
    categorical(["Firmware oscillation"; "Weak grid (bypass)"; "Weak grid flicker"; ...
                  "Degraded AFE"; "Buggy firmware fleet"; "MIMO grid-load interaction"]), ...
    ["Forced resonance at Kp < 50 W/V"; ...
     "Grid disturbances propagate to PFC DC buses"; ...
     "PCC voltage stress increases at low SCR"; ...
     "Load fluctuations amplified on UPS 800V DC bus"; ...
     sprintf("Poorly damped >%.0f%% penetration; unstable >%.0f%%", s.ImpedanceScan.frac_marginal*100, s.ImpedanceScan.frac_critical*100); ...
     sprintf("Eigenvalue encirclement at SCR<=5; d=%.2f at SCR=10, 90%% load", gnc_sweep{end}.distTable(1))], ...
    ["Enforce Kp > Kp_crit; UPS cannot compensate"; ...
     "Never bypass at SCR < 5"; ...
     "SCR >= 5 for adequate flicker margins"; ...
     "Monitor AFE controller health"; ...
     sprintf("Stage rollouts < %d units (%.0f%%)", s.ImpedanceScan.N_marginal, s.ImpedanceScan.frac_marginal*100); ...
     "Require SCR >= 10; monitor damping at high utilization"], ...
    VariableNames=["Risk", "Impact", "Design Rule"]);
disp(T_conclusion) %[output:6b7a5d5e]
%[text] Four failure categories govern stability, each with its own mitigation:
%[text] **(1) Firmware quality (SISO, 1–3 Hz, analytical).** CPL instability on the 400V DC bus — poorly damped above $\\sim$80% buggy penetration, unstable above $\\sim$95%. Grid-independent.
%[text] **(2) Grid-load interaction (MIMO GNC, 7–20 Hz, PRBS-measured).** Eigenvalue instability at the 480V PCC — stable only at SCR $\\geq$ 10 ($d \\approx 0.5$), unstable at SCR $\\leq$ 5. Firmware-independent (measured with nominal $K\_p$).
%[text] **(3) UPS controller health.** Degraded AFE amplifies PFC oscillations instead of absorbing them.
%[text] **(4) Joint constraint.** Each analysis assumes the other's variable is nominal. The combined worst case (buggy firmware + weak grid) is uncharacterized — the individual thresholds (firmware $\<$ 80%, SCR $\\geq$ 10, healthy AFE) are necessary conditions, to be enforced simultaneously.

